./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:5:## Harnessing the power of single-cell large language models with parameter-efficient fine-tuning using scPEFT
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:17:We systematically investigated and summarized the intended tasks and available official scripts for each scLLM included in our study, as presented in Supplementary Table 1 . It is demonstrated that  all  published  scLLMs  incorporate  cell  type  identification  pipelines  in  both  zero-shot  and finetuning  modes,  allowing  for  a  fair  and  consistent  benchmarking  of  scPEFT  across  scLLM backbones using this common downstream task.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:19:While we acknowledge that each scLLM (with the exception of scBERT, which is solely designed for  cell  type  identification)  was  originally  developed  for  specific  tasks  beyond  cell  type identification, some of these original tasks are often built upon zero-shot embeddings or attention maps derived from pretrained models (e.g., Geneformer's in silico gene perturbation, scGPT's gene  regulatory  network  inference,  and  scFoundation's  gene  module  discovery),  and  do  not include official finetuning protocols, making it challenging to compare adaptation strategies like scPEFT in a standardized manner.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:85:scBERT .  We incorporated scBERT 24 as the backbone for scPEFT in our conditional cell type identification and cross-species benchmarking, utilizing its provided cell type identification script with default hyperparameters. Unlike other scLLMs, scBERT standardizes input cell lengths to its gene vocabulary by padding non-existent genes with zero expression values and concatenating all gene embeddings into its projector module. This design results in a significantly larger number of parameters and increased training time compared to other scLLMs. Hence, we implemented the most lightweight Prefix adapter in scPEFT as the default adapter for its benchmarks in this study.  Detailed  hyperparameter  settings  are  provided  in Supplementary  Table  3 and  the recommended adapters for various downstream tasks are summarized in Supplementary Note 10 .
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:87:Geneformer .  We  used  Geneformer 25 as  the  backbone  for  scPEFT  in  transcription  factor identification, conditional cell type identification, and cross-species benchmarking, employing its provided gene classification and cell type identification scripts along with the 12-layer pretrained model.  Detailed  hyperparameter  settings  are  listed  in Supplementary  Table  3 ,  and  the recommended adapters for various downstream tasks are summarized in Supplementary Note 10 .
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:89:scGPT .  We  incorporated  scGPT 3 as  the  backbone  for  scPEFT  in  our  conditional  cell  type identification, cross-species benchmarking (using its provided cell type identification script), and novel  cell  group  discovery  (using  its  unsupervised  pretraining  script).  In  all  experiments,  we applied the recommended pretrained model trained on the whole human cell atlas, except for the near-context model comparison, where lung- and pan-cancer-specific pretrained models were used.  Detailed  hyperparameter  settings  are  provided  in Supplementary  Table  3 and  the recommended adapters for various downstream tasks are summarized in Supplementary Note 10 .
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:91:scFoundation . We incorporated scFoundation 26 as the backbone for scPEFT in our conditional cell  type  identification  (using  the  same  settings  as  in  scGPT)  and  perturbation  prediction benchmarking (using its provided perturbation prediction script, which connects it to GEARS). In all experiments, we used the provided 12-layer pretrained model weights. Detailed hyperparameter settings are listed in Supplementary Table 3 , and the recommended adapters for various downstream tasks are summarized in Supplementary Note 10 .
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:117:###### Supplementary Note 5: Additional hyperparameter sensitivity analysis of scPEFT
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:119:To further assess scPEFT's sensitivity to hyperparameters, we expanded the analysis originally presented  in  Fig.  3e  to  include  a  broader  range  of  tasks  and  datasets,  using  scGPT  as  the backbone model.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:121:First, beyond the M.S. dataset, we evaluated two additional datasets for the cell type identification task. As in Fig. 3e, we varied the number of hidden layers in each adapter. Across all settings and datasets, we observed consistent performance (see Supplementary Figs . 9ab ), with average accuracy variance remaining below  5%,  confirming the robustness of scPEFT  to  this hyperparameter.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:123:Next, we conducted similar hyperparameter sensitivity analyses for two other adaptation tasks supported by scGPT, including perturbation response prediction and batch effect correction. For perturbation response prediction ( Supplementary Figs. 9c-9f ), we focused on the mean squared error at the top 20 differentially expressed genes (MSE\_DE), a sensitive and task-relevant metric. Across all tested hyperparameter configurations and datasets, only the LoRA adapter showed notable sensitivity, with an MSE\_DE variance of 0.08 on the Adamson dataset. All other adapters exhibited minimal variation (MSE\_DE variance &lt; 0.03). Although a few configurations slightly underperformed fully finetuned scGPT, scPEFT still offered significant efficiency advantages due to the substantially reduced number of learnable parameters.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:127:These consistent results across tasks and datasets support our claim that scPEFT is insensitive to adapter hyperparameter configurations when using scGPT. Based on these observations, we selected  a  default  configuration  that  offers  a  substantial  trade-off  between  performance  and computational efficiency for each adapter in scPEFT (highlighted in Fig. 3e in the main text and the Supplementary Fig. 9 ). This default setting was then applied across all other experiments involving different scLLM backbones in this study, without further hyperparameter tuning.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:129:###### Supplementary Note 6: Runtime analysis of scPEFT
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:131:To evaluate the time efficiency of scLLM utility, we conducted runtime benchmarks comparing all adapter strategies in scPEFT against traditional finetuning across four different scLLM backbones and  diverse  downstream  tasks,  as  summarized  in Supplementary  Table 4 .  Notably,  all benchmarks were computed based on query cell time consumption, regardless of whether the tasks were at the gene level. For the cell type identification task, scBERT inputs the entire gene panel rather than just the expressed genes, resulting in  an additional excitation time. For TF identification, even though transcription factors (TFs) are a small proportion of genes, Geneformer still needs to input whole transcriptomics for gene contextual learning and mask out non-TFs in its inference operation, leading to a longer time than its cell type identification consumption. For the perturbation response prediction task using scPEFT with the scFoundation backbone, the reported runtimes include additional  computation  time  attributable  to  GEARS  alongside scFoundation.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:133:Our  observations  indicate  that  while  scPEFT  substantially  reduces  the  number  of  trainable parameters,  this  reduction  does  not  always  translate  directly  into  faster  inference  times. Compared to zero-shot inference or finetuned scLLM models, scPEFT with adapters incurs a slight increase in execution time per sample (whether a gene or a cell, depending on the task). This overhead is due to the additional computations introduced by the plug-in adapters during the inference  stage  alongside  the  original  scLLM.  For  most  gene-  and  cell-level  tasks,  this  extra computation ranges from approximately 0.1 to 0.3 milliseconds, depending on the backbone and task, which is negligible and practically imperceptible to end users. Meanwhile, scPEFT maintains significant advantages in terms of reduced graphics processing unit (GPU) memory usage, lower requirements for annotated data, and faster convergence during model adaptation, as illustrated in Fig. 3 of the main text.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:135:###### Supplementary Note 7: Cell-group-specific analysis of top 30 genes identified by scPEFT attention
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:137:To  provide  further  evidence  of  the  enhanced  disease  gene  enrichment  achieved  by  scPEFT relative to both native and finetuned scLLMs via attention-based analysis, we additionally report the fold-change (FC) values for the top 30 attention-ranked genes identified by scPEFT in the NSCLC and COVID-19 datasets, thereby complementing our prior evaluations of disease gene detection.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:141:Overall, genes ranked by attention in native scGPT, finetuned scGPT, and scPEFT all showed varying levels of specificity to the target CD4 ⁺ T cell subtypes. Notably, scPEFT demonstrated an enhanced ability to identify TFs (e.g., CREM in Supplementary Fig. 10 , FOS in Supplementary Fig. 13 , FOXP3 in Supplementary Fig. 17 ) that are potentially relevant but often expressed at low  levels.  These  genes  are  typically  overlooked  by  traditional  differential  expression  (DEG) methods, but scPEFT's contextualized attention mechanism  enables  their prioritization, suggesting an advantage of deep attention-based modeling. Most notable were the regulatory T cells  ( Supplementary Fig. 17 ),  where  scPEFT  was  the  only  method  to  prioritize  well-known transcription factors ( FOXP3 ) and immunomodulatory molecules ( CTLA4 , TIGIT , IL2RA ). It also highlighted several genes with intriguing potential for new insights into T reg biology that have not yet been reported. Some of these genes were also among the lower-expressed genes.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:143:In  the  FC  comparisons  for  CD4 + T  cell  subtypes ISG ( Supplementary  Fig.  14 ), Th1 -like ( Supplementary Fig. 16 ),  and Proliferation ( Supplementary Fig. 18 ),  attention-ranked genes from finetuned scGPT failed to consistently exhibit strong subtype specificity, whereas both native scGPT and scPEFT maintained clearer specificity. Moreover, scPEFT often prioritized genes with lower expression frequency but higher cell-group specificity (e.g., in T em , CD69 , Th1-like , T reg , and Proliferation  of  CD4 + T  cell  subtypes),  which  were  likely  missed  by  both  DEG  analysis  and attention analysis using native or finetuned scLLMs.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:145:A similar trend was observed in the COVID-19 dataset ( Supplementary Figs. 19-20 ), where the top 30 attention-ranked genes from scPEFT demonstrated strong specificity to relevant cell states. Among all evaluated models, scPEFT consistently enriched COVID-associated genes at higher ranks  (as  shown  in Fig.  4 in  the  main  text  and  highlighted  in Supplementary Figs.  19-20 ), improving  the  identification  of  cell-state-specific  genes  without  introducing  spurious  or  nonspecific hits. In summary, scPEFT not only improves disease gene enrichment but also better maintains gene expression specificity, especially for low expression yet biologically meaningful genes, thereby supporting its robustness for disease gene discovery.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:147:Altogether, these results show that scPEFT can identify key regulators and cell state-defining genes. This approach overcomes statistical limitations in detecting regulators with low expression but strong cell state-driving potential.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:149:###### Supplementary Note 8: Unsupervised adaptation of scPEFT to the lung cancer atlas
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:153:In  this  experiment, only the gene expression profiles were used as input to the unsupervised adaptation protocol. The resulting cell embeddings generated by native scGPT, finetuned scGPT, and scPEFT were visualized using UMAP, with cell colors reflecting the hierarchical annotations. This  allowed  us  to  assess  how  well  each  model  captured  cellular  structure  and  revealed subpopulations within the disease atlas.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:155:From the Uniform Manifold Approximation and Projection UMAP visualizations ( Supplementary Fig. 21a ), we observed clear clustering of major cell types, even in the zero-shot embeddings from native scGPT, highlighting the strong biological prior captured during pretraining. Following adaptation, both finetuned scGPT and scPEFT further refined the separation of major cell types and  introduced  additional  subclusters.  Higher  Calinski-Harabasz  index  (CHI)  scores  for  the adapted  models  confirmed  improved  cluster  separability  and  cohesiveness.  These  emergent subclusters suggest that context-specific biological signals were captured during unsupervised adaptation.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:157:To further investigate these subpopulations, we applied finer annotations to color the major cell types ( Supplementary Fig. 21b ). Within the epithelial compartment, which includes tumor cells, scPEFT  and  finetuned  scGPT  revealed  clear  boundaries  between  alveolar,  transitional,  and cancer cells, separations not apparent in native scGPT embeddings, indicating that the adapted models generated more context-aware representations. Moreover, the cancer cell population split into numerous distinct subclusters, each corresponding to its patient of origin and associated with varying Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) stages ( Supplementary Fig. 21c ). This pattern  is  expected  because  the  profile  of  genomic  alterations  in  each  tumor  will  be  unique, making a spread of augmentations to the baseline gene expression state.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:159:This trend was consistently observed across other major cell types with finer cell type annotations in  the  atlas  ( Supplementary  Figs.  22-23 ).  Across  these  cases,  scPEFT  generally  achieved higher CHI scores than finetuned scGPT, particularly for dominant cell groups, including CD4 + T cells,  alveolar  macrophages,  and  lymphatic  endothelial  cells.  These  results  suggest  that compared to the finetuning strategy, scPEFT better preserves the overall structure of cell types while  offering  discerning  sensitivity  to  biologically  meaningful  subpopulations  in  large-scale disease contexts.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:165:From  the  benchmarking  results  from  scFoundation  backbone  ( Supplementary Table 5 ),  the linear model achieved the best or near-best PCC and MSE values across the two datasets. This finding  is  consistent  with  prior  work 31 ,  which  demonstrated  that  general  purpose  scLLM representations  of  cellular  states  struggle  to  accurately  predict  perturbation  effects  across  all genes even after adaptation. However, when the evaluation is restricted to the top differentially expressed  genes,  which  carry  stronger  biological  signals  and  are  more  likely  to  serve  as biomarkers driving phenotypic changes 32,33 , the linear model consistently underperformed relative to scPEFT. Specifically, scPEFT outperformed the linear model by 0.02 to 0.09 in MSE\_DE and 0.02 to 0.15 in PCC\_DE, while the linear model performed comparably to zero-shot scFoundation on  the  datasets.  These  results  suggest  that  scPEFT  is  able  to  capture  the  strong  non-linear perturbation  patterns  from  adapted  data,  which  the  linear  model  cannot  capture  and  which general-purpose zero-shot scFoundation cannot provide. A similar trend was observed in the benchmark using the scGPT  backbone ( Supplementary Table  6 ). The linear model demonstrated  higher  performance  in  terms  of  PCC  and  MSE  across  four  datasets,  but  it consistently lagged behind in MSE\_DE and PCC\_DE.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:167:Nevertheless, the improvement achieved by these deep learning-based models relative to the linear model is modest when considering their higher computational cost. A potential explanation suggested is that pretraining on single-cell atlas data provides only limited benefit for perturbation prediction. This challenges the assumption of scPEFT that a generalizable embedding space for any target task is already established during the pretraining stage of scLLMs. Notably, a proof-ofconcept study using the linear model 31 indicates that pretraining on perturbation data can improve predictive performance. In our future work, we plan to extend scPEFT the specific scLLM STATE 34 , pretrained on perturbation data, to further enhance its adaptability for this open challenge.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:169:###### Supplementary Note 10: Best practices for applying scPEFT across scLLM backbones and downstream tasks
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:171:Providing a best practice summary of scPEFT configurations across different scLLM backbones and downstream tasks is essential to enhance its practical utility and reduce trial-and-error costs for users. Accordingly, we compiled the best-performing individual adapter configurations for all scLLMs, and tasks studied here, as summarized in Supplementary Table 7 .
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:179:It  is  important  to  note  that  the  recommendations  in Supplementary  Table  7 are  based  on scenarios with official scripts provided by the respective scLLM releases. We plan to continuously extend  scPEFT  support  to  untested  and  emerging  single-cell  analysis  scenarios  and  provide updated best practice guidance in future work.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:181:###### Supplementary Note 11: Adapter combinations and configuration in scPEFT
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:191:The recent research on PEFT techniques concentrates on improvement upon LoRA. For instance, AdLoRA 35 dynamically  allocates  LoRA's  low-rank  matrices  across  layers  based  on  their  taskspecific  importance,  rather  than  applying  them  uniformly.  PISSA 36 only  updates  the  principal singular  values  and  singular  vectors  of  weight  matrices  in  LoRA.  DoRA 37 decomposes LoRA weight updates into direction and magnitude, only finetuning the direction while preserving the pre-trained magnitude. Among LoRA variants, we selected two representative and competitive LoRA variants, Orthogonal Fine-Tuning (OFT) 38 and Householder Reflection Adaptation (HRA) 39 , to conduct a comparison with scPEFT. These two methods employ different matrix formulations to replace the low-rank decomposition used in LoRA, aiming to reduce parameter counts and enhance representational capacity.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:211:Both OFT and HRA achieved slightly higher accuracy and lower variance than LoRA, confirming their improvements and stability. These results demonstrate that reducing the parameter space does not necessarily compromise task performance and can even enhance model robustness. However,  scPEFT's  default  encoder  adapter  still  achieved  the  highest  average  accuracy, consistently outperforming OFT and HRA by 1-5% across all three datasets. This indicates that, even compared to state-of-the-art PEFT methods, scPEFT remains highly competitive.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:213:These results validate that scPEFT is competitive with recent PEFT approaches and highlight its robustness  and  effectiveness  for  single-cell  tasks.  Furthermore,  scPEFT  is  designed  as  an extensible framework, and we have integrated support for both OFT and HRA to allow flexible adapter configurations for diverse use cases. As a modular PEFT framework for scLLMs, scPEFT is compatible with emerging techniques and will continue to evolve with future developments in the field. We are committed to maintaining and expanding scPEFT to support more downstream tasks and benefit the broader single-cell research community.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:215:###### Supplementary Note 13: Anticipated challenges in extending scPEFT to future scLLMs
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:217:Although  scPEFT  is  designed  to  be  flexible  and  compatible  with  future  scLLMs  because  its adapters operate externally to the backbone models, several practical challenges may still arise. One specific challenge we encountered is the diversity in implementation details and software dependencies  among  existing  scLLMs.  For  instance,  Geneformer  was  developed  using  the Hugging  Face  framework,  which  abstracts  many  low-level  details  such  as  tokenization  and encoder design. This abstraction required extra engineering efforts when attempting to integrate Token  and  Encoder  adapters  from  scPEFT.  In  contrast,  scGPT  and  scFoundation  are implemented using both standard PyTorch Transformer components and a customized version based on FlashAttention. This variation requires programming expertise to properly insert the scPEFT adapters.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:219:In  addition  to  these  technical  considerations,  we  are  observing  a  rapid  development  of  new scLLMs to support a broader range of single-cell applications, such as spatial transcriptomics and multi-omics  data  analysis.  As  the  fields  of  both  scLLMs  and  Parameter-Efficient  Fine-Tuning (PEFT) methods continueessentia to evolve, it will be important to actively update and extend scPEFT to remain compatible with new models and use cases. Despite these challenges, we will be committed to making future scLLMs more accessible for various single-cell applications while reducing the computational burden, thereby benefiting a broader research community, including groups with limited resources.
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.md:335:Supplementary Table 4: Runtime comparison between native, finetuning scLLMs and scPEFT with different adapters. These comparisons were conducted on machines equipped with 2.3GHz processors, 300 GB physical memory, and an NVIDIA A100 GPU with 40 GB memory. N/A indicates that no official script is available from the respective scLLM for the given downstream task.
