./analysis/fulltext_uncertain_disagreement_current_llm_20260512/current_6_complete_uncertain_or_disagreement_fulltext_targets.csv:792:1477,Harnessing the Power of Single-Cell Large Language Models with Parameter Efficient Fine-Tuning using scPEFT,10.1038/s42256-025-01170-z,,40313770.0,,,2025.0,bioRxiv,"He et al. present a parameter-efficient fine-tuning method for single-cell language models that improves performance on unseen diseases, treatments and cell types. Single-cell large language models (scLLMs) capture essential biological insights from vast single-cell atlases but struggle in out-of-context applications, where zero-shot predictions can be unreliable. To address this, here we introduce a single-cell parameter-efficient fine-tuning (scPEFT) framework that integrates learnable, low-dimensional adapters into scLLMs. By freezing the backbone model and updating only the adapter parameters, scPEFT efficiently adapts to specific tasks using limited custom data. This approach mitigates catastrophic forgetting, reduces parameter tuning by over 96% and decreases GPU memory usage by more than half, thus substantially enhancing the accessibility of scLLMs for resource-constrained researchers. When validated across diverse datasets, scPEFT outperformed zero-shot models and traditional fine-tuning in disease-specific, cross-species and undercharacterized cell population tasks. Its attention-mechanism analysis identified COVID-related genes associated with specific cell states and uncovered unique blood cell subpopulations, demonstrating the capacity of scPEFT for condition-specific interpretations. These findings position scPEFT as an efficient solution for enhancing the utility of scLLMs in general single-cell analyses.","['google_scholar', 'semantic_scholar', 'springernature']",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,UNCERTAIN,EXCLUDE
./analysis/fulltext_uncertain_disagreement_current_llm_20260512/current_6_complete_uncertain_or_disagreement_fulltext_targets.csv:795:1500,Language-Enhanced Representation Learning for Single-Cell Transcriptomics,,,,2503.09427,,2025.0,,"Single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) offers detailed insights into cellular heterogeneity. Recent advancements leverage single-cell large language models (scLLMs) for effective representation learning. These models focus exclusively on transcriptomic data, neglecting complementary biological knowledge from textual descriptions. To overcome this limitation, we propose scMMGPT, a novel multimodal framework designed for language-enhanced representation learning in single-cell transcriptomics. Unlike existing methods, scMMGPT employs robust cell representation extraction, preserving quantitative gene expression data, and introduces an innovative two-stage pre-training strategy combining discriminative precision with generative flexibility. Extensive experiments demonstrate that scMMGPT significantly outperforms unimodal and multimodal baselines across key downstream tasks, including cell annotation and clustering, and exhibits superior generalization in out-of-distribution scenarios.","['arxiv', 'google_scholar', 'semantic_scholar']",UNCERTAIN,UNCERTAIN,EXCLUDE,UNCERTAIN,UNCERTAIN,UNCERTAIN
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/deepseek_v4_review_pipeline_all4027_current_rep2_watchdog_20260510T013948Z/job_11535/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.jsonl:267:{"cluster_id": 4044, "title": "Evaluating the learnability of single-cell large language models on multiple tasks", "title_normalized": "evaluating the learnability of singlecell large language models on multiple tasks", "doi": NaN, "preprint_doi": NaN, "pmid": NaN, "arxiv_id": NaN, "abstract": "Abstract\n                The rise of single-cell foundation models (scFMs) has sparked interest in their potential to unify diverse biological tasks. However, their practical utility and the validity of scaling laws—the assumption that performance improves with model and data size—remain under-examined. Here, we systematically evaluate two representative scFMs, Geneformer and scGPT, across perturbation prediction and cell type annotation tasks. Our findings suggest that the benefits of large-scale pretraining are strongly task-dependent, conferring substantial advantages in cell type annotation but limited gains in perturbation prediction. Furthermore, our results indicate that increasing model size does not guarantee improved performance and can even be detrimental, challenging the ``bigger is better'' paradigm. By comparing model performance on real versus synthetic data with different levels of complexity, our analysis suggests that for perturbation prediction, the tested scFMs capture little more than simple summary statistics and may struggle to learn complex biological interactions. These results highlight the need to move beyond scaling and toward developing models that integrate deeper biological knowledge. We suggest that a renewed focus on task-specific architectures and biologically-informed priors may be critical for unlocking the true potential of foundation models in single-cell biology.", "authors": "['Y Yan', 'X Wang', 'D Song']", "year": 2026.0, "venue": NaN, "date": NaN, "url": "https://www.researchsquare.com/article/rs-8919408/latest", "sources": "['google_scholar']", "n_sources": 1, "all_dois": "[]", "duplicate_count": 1, "abstract_source": NaN, "abstract_enriched_from": "crossref_title", "abstract_old_len": 188.0, "update_batch": "2026-03-01_to_2026-04-14", "_pilot_row_id": 288, "round-A_scope_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "benchmark_resource", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "no", "text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "primary_exclusion_code": "benchmark_resource", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "none", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "decision_rationale": "The abstract evaluates existing scFMs (Geneformer, scGPT) on tasks; it is a benchmark/resource paper, not a new model. No natural-language text component is described."}, "round-A_scope_reviewer_paper_type": "benchmark_resource", "round-A_scope_reviewer_bio_modality_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_component_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "benchmark_resource", "round-A_scope_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "round-A_scope_reviewer_boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "round-A_scope_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract evaluates existing scFMs (Geneformer, scGPT) on tasks; it is a benchmark/resource paper, not a new model. No natural-language text component is described.", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "benchmark_resource", "generative_model_present": "no", "foundation_model_evidence": "no", "primary_exclusion_code": "benchmark_resource", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component":