./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/deepseek_v4_review_pipeline_all4027_current_rep2_watchdog_20260510T013948Z/job_11505/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.jsonl:76:{"cluster_id": 1477, "title": "Harnessing the Power of Single-Cell Large Language Models with Parameter Efficient Fine-Tuning using scPEFT", "title_normalized": "harnessing the power of singlecell large language models with parameter efficient finetuning using scpeft", "doi": "10.1038/s42256-025-01170-z", "preprint_doi": NaN, "pmid": 40313770.0, "arxiv_id": NaN, "abstract": "He et al. present a parameter-efficient fine-tuning method for single-cell language models that improves performance on unseen diseases, treatments and cell types. Single-cell large language models (scLLMs) capture essential biological insights from vast single-cell atlases but struggle in out-of-context applications, where zero-shot predictions can be unreliable. To address this, here we introduce a single-cell parameter-efficient fine-tuning (scPEFT) framework that integrates learnable, low-dimensional adapters into scLLMs. By freezing the backbone model and updating only the adapter parameters, scPEFT efficiently adapts to specific tasks using limited custom data. This approach mitigates catastrophic forgetting, reduces parameter tuning by over 96% and decreases GPU memory usage by more than half, thus substantially enhancing the accessibility of scLLMs for resource-constrained researchers. When validated across diverse datasets, scPEFT outperformed zero-shot models and traditional fine-tuning in disease-specific, cross-species and undercharacterized cell population tasks. Its attention-mechanism analysis identified COVID-related genes associated with specific cell states and uncovered unique blood cell subpopulations, demonstrating the capacity of scPEFT for condition-specific interpretations. These findings position scPEFT as an efficient solution for enhancing the utility of scLLMs in general single-cell analyses.", "authors": "Fei He; Ruixin Fei; Jordan E. Krull; Xinyu Zhang; Mingyue Gao; Li Su; Yibo Chen; Yang Yu; Jinpu Li; Baichuan Jin; Yuzhou Chang; A. Ma; Qin Ma; Dong Xu", "year": 2025.0, "venue": "bioRxiv", "date": "2025-04-23", "url": NaN, "sources": "['google_scholar', 'semantic_scholar', 'springernature']", "n_sources": 3, "all_dois": "['10.1038/s42256-025-01170-z']", "duplicate_count": 3, "abstract_source": NaN, "abstract_enriched_from": NaN, "abstract_old_len": NaN, "update_batch": NaN, "_pilot_row_id": 28, "round-A_scope_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "unclear", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "no", "text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "primary_exclusion_code": "EC2_no_text_component", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "none", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "decision_rationale": "The abstract describes a fine-tuning method (scPEFT) for single-cell large language models, which are biological-token models, not natural-language text models. No text component or text-bio bridge is mentioned, so the record is excluded."}, "round-A_scope_reviewer_paper_type": "unclear", "round-A_scope_reviewer_bio_modality_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_component_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "EC2_no_text_component", "round-A_scope_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "round-A_scope_reviewer_boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "round-A_scope_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract describes a fine-tuning method (scPEFT) for single-cell large language models, which are biological-token models, not natural-language text models. No text component or text-bio bridge is mentioned, so the record is excluded.", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "application_wrapper", "generative_model_present": "no", "foundation_model_evidence": "no", "primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "boundary_case": "wrapper_boundary", "decision_rationale": "The abstract describes scPEFT as a parameter-efficient fine-tuning method for existing single-cell large language models, not as a new generative architecture. The paper's contribution is a wrapper around pre-existing generative models, with no description of a generative mechanism for the candidate model."}, "round-A_architecture_reviewer_paper_type": "application_wrapper", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_generative_model_present": "no", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_foundation_model_evidence": "no", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_boundary_case": "wrapper_boundary", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract describes scPEFT as a parameter-efficient fine-tuning method for existing single-cell large language models, not as a new generative architecture. The paper's contribution is a wrapper around pre-existing generative models, with no description of a generative mechanism for the candidate model.", "pilot_roundA_scope_gate": "UNCERTAIN", "pilot_roundA_architecture_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_needs_adjudication": true, "round-B_adjudicator_output": {"paper_type": "application_wrapper", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "no", "text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "generative_model_present": "no", "foundation_model_evidence": "unclear", "primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "none", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "boundary_case": "wrapper_boundary", "decision_rationale": "The abstract presents scPEFT as a parameter-efficient fine-tuning method for existing single-cell language models, not a new generative model or one with natural-language text component. It is an application wrapper for adapting scLLMs, thus excluded as application_wrapper."}, "round-B_adjudicator_paper_type": "application_wrapper", "round-B_adjudicator_bio_modality_present": "yes", "round-B_adjudicator_text_component_present": "no", "round-B_adjudicator_text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "round-B_adjudicator_generative_model_present": "no", "round-B_adjudicator_foundation_model_evidence": "unclear", "round-B_adjudicator_primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "round-B_adjudicator_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-B_adjudicator_evidence_for_text_component": "none", "round-B_adjudicator_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "round-B_adjudicator_evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "round-B_adjudicator_boundary_case": "wrapper_boundary", "round-B_adjudicator_decision_rationale": "The abstract presents scPEFT as a parameter-efficient fine-tuning method for existing single-cell language models, not a new generative model or one with natural-language text component. It is an application wrapper for adapting scLLMs, thus excluded as application_wrapper."}
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/deepseek_v4_review_pipeline_all4027_current_rep2_watchdog_20260510T013948Z/job_11505/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.jsonl:96:{"cluster_id": 1500, "title": "Language-Enhanced Representation Learning for Single-Cell Transcriptomics", "title_normalized": "languageenhanced representation learning for singlecell transcriptomics", "doi": NaN, "preprint_doi": NaN, "pmid": NaN, "arxiv_id": "2503.09427", "abstract": "Single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) offers detailed insights into cellular heterogeneity. Recent advancements leverage single-cell large language models (scLLMs) for effective representation learning. These models focus exclusively on transcriptomic data, neglecting complementary biological knowledge from textual descriptions. To overcome this limitation, we propose scMMGPT, a novel multimodal framework designed for language-enhanced representation learning in single-cell transcriptomics. Unlike existing methods, scMMGPT employs robust cell representation extraction, preserving quantitative gene expression data, and introduces an innovative two-stage pre-training strategy combining discriminative precision with generative flexibility. Extensive experiments demonstrate that scMMGPT significantly outperforms unimodal and multimodal baselines across key downstream tasks, including cell annotation and clustering, and exhibits superior generalization in out-of-distribution scenarios.", "authors": "Yaorui Shi; Jiaqi Yang; Chang-Sheng Nai; Sihang Li; Junfeng Fang; Xiang Wang; Zhiyuan Liu; Yang Zhang", "year": 2025.0, "venue": NaN, "date": "2025-03-12", "url": NaN, "sources": "['arxiv', 'google_scholar', 'semantic_scholar']", "n_sources": 3, "all_dois": "[]", "duplicate_count": 3, "abstract_source": NaN, "abstract_enriched_from": NaN, "abstract_old_len": NaN, "update_batch": NaN, "_pilot_row_id": 51, "round-A_scope_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "bio_modality_present": "