lay: none;\n            }l modality but not a text/language component.","evidence_snippet":"\"A Universal Foundation Model for Single-Cell Chromatin Accessibiltiy Data\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000254","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title presents ChromFound as a universal foundation model for single-cell chromatin accessibility data. The abstract snippet does not give enough mechanism detail to confirm a generative architecture.","evidence_snippet":"“Towards A Universal Foundation Model for Single-Cell Chromatin Accessibiltiy Data”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000256","title":"AICellType: a large language model-based platform for accurate cell type annotation","abstract":"Abstract\n                  Accurate cell type annotation is critical for studying cellular heterogeneity in single-cell and spatial transcriptomics. However, existing methods largely rely on static gene markers, limiting adaptability to diverse biological contexts and data types. To overcome this limitation, we systematically benchmarked 79 large language models (LLMs) over 1130 single-cell and spatial transcriptomics datasets using an evaluation framework combining ontology structure and semantic reasoning to quantify model performance in biological relevance and annotation robustness. Claude 3.5 Sonnet achieved the best overall performance, balancing weighted accuracy (76%), robustness, inference speed, and cost-efficiency. Based on these findings, we developed AICellType (https://AICellType.jinlab.online), a free, open-source R package and web platform that integrates seamlessly with Seurat workflows, supports multiple species and tissues, and enables flexible model deployment via OpenRouter or custom APIs. By leveraging LLMs’ capacity to interpret marker–cell type associations, AICellType provides a scalable, efficient, and accessible solution for real-world cell annotation in both single-cell and spatial omics research.","doi":"10.1093/bib/bbag151","year":"2026","venue":"Briefings in …","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000256","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract centers on benchmarking 79 large language models across 1130 transcriptomics datasets and releasing a platform. This is primarily a benchmark/resource and application platform paper, not a new candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"\"systematically benchmarked 79 large language models\" and \"developed AICellType ... a free, open-source R package and web platform\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000256","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a benchmarking study over 79 LLMs and a deployed platform for cell type annotation. It does not present a new generative foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"“we systematically benchmarked 79 large language models” and “developed AICellType ... a ... platform”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"benchmark_resource","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}}]
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0026.stderr.log:2969:./data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0012.stderr.log:139:{"results":[{"record_id":"rec_000240","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_substantive_text_bio_bridge","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes agentic LLM schemes for authoring genomics visualizations, not a new text-bio foundation model. This is wrapper-style use of existing LLMs for visualization generation.","evidence_snippet":"\"Agentic and large language model (LLM) approaches\" and \"All schemes use the Gosling visualization grammar as structured output\""},{"record_id":"rec_000249","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Although the abstract uses scLLMs, the contribution is a parameter-efficient fine-tuning framework around an existing backbone, not a new foundation model. The reviewer conflict is resolved conservatively as an application wrapper.","evidence_snippet":"\"single-cell parameter-efficient fine-tuning (scPEFT) framework\" and \"freezing the backbone model\""},{"record_id":"rec_000250","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title and abstract identify scpFormer as a transformer-based foundation model for single-cell proteomics, but the record does not show any text or language component. The generative mechanism is not specified in the snippet.","evidence_snippet":"\"a transformer-based foundation model designed for single-cell proteomics\" and \"Pre-trained on\""},{"record_id":"rec_000253","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title states this is a gene set foundation model pre-trained on diverse gene sets, so it fits as a primary biological model. The abstract excerpt does not establish a substantive text component or a clear generative architecture.","evidence_snippet":"\"gene set foundation model pre-trained on a massive collection of diverse gene sets\""},{"record_id":"rec_000254","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"ChromFound is presented as a universal foundation model for single-cell chromatin accessibility data, which supports a primary biological model classification. The provided text does not indicate a text component or enough mechanism detail to confirm generation.","evidence_snippet":"\"Towards A Universal Foundation Model for Single-Cell Chromatin Accessibiltiy Data\""},{"record_id":"rec_000256","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract centers on benchmarking 79 LLMs over transcriptomics datasets and releasing AICellType as a platform, so this is a benchmark/resource paper rather than a candidate model. The biological text-to-cell annotation bridge is present, but the paper is not a new generative foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"\"systematically benchmarked 79 large language models\" and \"developed AICellType ... a free, open-source R package and web platform\""}]}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0026.stderr.log:3163:/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/adjudicator.jsonl:68:{"record_id": "rec_000249", "paper_type": "application_wrapper", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "yes", "text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "generative_model_present": "yes", "foundation_model_evidence": "no", "primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "decision_rationale": "Although the abstract uses scLLMs, the contribution is a parameter-efficient fine-tuning framework around an existing backbone, not a new foundation model. The reviewer conflict is resolved conservatively as an application wrapper.", "evidence_snippet": "\"single-cell parameter-efficient fine-tuning (scPEFT) framework\" and \"freezing the backbone model\""}
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206.jsonl:17:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_003206", "chunk_index": 17, "text": "More recently, efforts such as the Gene Expression Omnibus (GEO) [21] and Human Cell Atlas (HCA) [22] have centralized and standardized the storage of data from hundreds of single-cell experiments across a wide range of tissues, comprising hundreds of millions of measurements. Several models have been designed and trained on this data (e.g. scGPT [23], scFoundation [24], Geneformer [25]), with the goal of creating a foundation model for single cell transcriptomics data analogous to foundation models in natural language processing.\nPrompt fine-tuning Since the introduction of GPT-2 [26], prompting has become a common method to elicit meaningful behavior from large language models ([27], [28], [29]). Recently, publicly available datasets like Alpaca [30] and dataset generators such as Flan [31], [32], [33] together with parameter efficient fine-tuning [34] have made it possible to train custom large language models. A survey of methods can be found in [35].", "contextualized_text": "1 Introduction\n1.1 Related Work\nMore recently, efforts such as the Gene Expression Omnibus (GEO) [21] and Human Cell Atlas (HCA) [22] have centralized and standardized the storage of data from hundreds of single-cell experiments across a wide range of tissues, comprising hundreds of millions of measurements. Several models have been designed and trained on this data (e.g. scGPT [23], scFoundation [24], Geneformer [25]), with the goal of creating a foundation model for single cell transcriptomics data analogous to foundation models in natural language processing.\nPrompt fine-tuning Since the introduction of GPT-2 [26], prompting has become a common method to elicit meaningful behavior from large language models ([27], [28], [29]). Recently, publicly available datasets like Alpaca [30] and dataset generators such as Flan [31], [32], [33] together with parameter efficient fine-tuning [34] have made it possible to train custom large language models. A survey of methods can be found in [35].", "headings": ["1 Introduction", "1.1 Related Work"], "pages": [3], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/47", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 3, "bbox": {"l": 107.670999, "t": 395.602913, "r": 505.76102191192314, "b": 332.50469143362835, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 536]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/48", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 3, "bbox": {"l": 108.0, "t": 311.93847, "r": 505.26306741188665, "b": 259.3606914336283, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 432]}]}], "headings": ["1 Introduction", "1.1 Related Work"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 4970474802907137880, "filename": "full_text_01_openalex_title_content_pdf.pdf", "uri": null}}}
