ning using scPEFT", "Supplementary Notes", "Supplementary Note 12: Comparisons with state-of-the-art PEFT techniques"], "pages": [18], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/116", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 18, "bbox": {"l": 72.0, "t": 370.78463999999997, "r": 543.295392, "b": 301.50830532596683, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 473]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/117", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 18, "bbox": {"l": 72.0, "t": 282.22463999999997, "r": 543.3130560000001, "b": 243.1883053259669, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 242]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/118", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "formula", "prov": [{"page_no": 18, "bbox": {"l": 147.67968, "t": 240.48263999999995, "r": 540.0216, "b": 197.9655304421052, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 117]}]}], "headings": ["Harnessing the power of single-cell large language models with parameter-efficient fine-tuning using scPEFT", "Supplementary Notes", "Supplementary Note 12: Comparisons with state-of-the-art PEFT techniques"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 3578786038470275027, "filename": "full_text_02_landing_pdf_link.pdf", "uri": null}}}
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.jsonl:58:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_001358", "chunk_index": 58, "text": "Where 𝑟 denotes the number of trainable householder reflections, 𝐼 is an identity matrix, 𝕊 =>$ is a d -dimensional  hypersphere,  each 𝑢 ! is  a  unit  normal  vector  on  the 𝕊 =>$ .  This  formulation requires only 𝑟 ∗ 𝑑 trainable parameters, where 𝑟 is much smaller than 𝑑 . The weight matrices in Key,  and  Value  components  followed  the  same  procedure  to  get  updated  in  the  adaptation process. We use the default 𝑟\t = \t8 as reported in the original HRA study.\nWe  benchmarked  OFT  and  HRA  using  the  cell  type  identification  task  with  scGPT  as  the backbone, keeping all other training settings consistent with our LoRA experiments. From the benchmarking results over three disease datasets ( Supplementary Fig. 25 ),", "contextualized_text": "Harnessing the power of single-cell large language models with parameter-efficient fine-tuning using scPEFT\nSupplementary Notes\nSupplementary Note 12: Comparisons with state-of-the-art PEFT techniques\nWhere 𝑟 denotes the number of trainable householder reflections, 𝐼 is an identity matrix, 𝕊 =>$ is a d -dimensional  hypersphere,  each 𝑢 ! is  a  unit  normal  vector  on  the 𝕊 =>$ .  This  formulation requires only 𝑟 ∗ 𝑑 trainable parameters, where 𝑟 is much smaller than 𝑑 . The weight matrices in Key,  and  Value  components  followed  the  same  procedure  to  get  updated  in  the  adaptation process. We use the default 𝑟\t = \t8 as reported in the original HRA study.\nWe  benchmarked  OFT  and  HRA  using  the  cell  type  identification  task  with  scGPT  as  the backbone, keeping all other training settings consistent with our LoRA experiments. From the benchmarking results over three disease datasets ( Supplementary Fig. 25 ),", "headings": ["Harnessing the power of single-cell large language models with parameter-efficient fine-tuning using scPEFT", "Supplementary Notes", "Supplementary Note 12: Comparisons with state-of-the-art PEFT techniques"], "pages": [18, 19], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/119", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 18, "bbox": {"l": 72.0, "t": 195.60263999999995, "r": 543.3263039999998, "b": 124.62830532596683, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 476]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/121", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 19, "bbox": {"l": 72.0, "t": 717.5846399999999, "r": 543.2048640000006, "b": 678.7883053259668, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 267]}]}], "headings": ["Harnessing the power of single-cell large language models with parameter-efficient fine-tuning using scPEFT", "Supplementary Notes", "Supplementary Note 12: Comparisons with state-of-the-art PEFT techniques"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 3578786038470275027, "filename": "full_text_02_landing_pdf_link.pdf", "uri": null}}}
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.jsonl:59:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_001358", "chunk_index": 59, "text": "Both OFT and HRA achieved slightly higher accuracy and lower variance than LoRA, confirming their improvements and stability. These results demonstrate that reducing the parameter space does not necessarily compromise task performance and can even enhance model robustness. However,  scPEFT's  default  encoder  adapter  still  achieved  the  highest  average  accuracy, consistently outperforming OFT and HRA by 1-5% across all three datasets. This indicates that, even compared to state-of-the-art PEFT methods, scPEFT remains highly competitive.", "contextualized_text": "Harnessing the power of single-cell large language models with parameter-efficient fine-tuning using scPEFT\nSupplementary Notes\nSupplementary Note 12: Comparisons with state-of-the-art PEFT techniques\nBoth OFT and HRA achieved slightly higher accuracy and lower variance than LoRA, confirming their improvements and stability. These results demonstrate that reducing the parameter space does not necessarily compromise task performance and can even enhance model robustness. However,  scPEFT's  default  encoder  adapter  still  achieved  the  highest  average  accuracy, consistently outperforming OFT and HRA by 1-5% across all three datasets. This indicates that, even compared to state-of-the-art PEFT methods, scPEFT remains highly competitive.", "headings": ["Harnessing the power of single-cell large language models with parameter-efficient fine-tuning using scPEFT", "Supplementary Notes", "Supplementary Note 12: Comparisons with state-of-the-art PEFT techniques"], "pages": [19], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/122", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 19, "bbox": {"l": 72.0, "t": 659.9846399999999, "r": 543.2912640000001, "b": 577.0283053259668, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 548]}]}], "headings": ["Harnessing the power of single-cell large language models with parameter-efficient fine-tuning using scPEFT", "Supplementary Notes", "Supplementary Note 12: Comparisons with state-of-the-art PEFT techniques"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 3578786038470275027, "filename": "full_text_02_landing_pdf_link.pdf", "uri": null}}}
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.jsonl:60:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_001358", "chunk_index": 60, "text": "These results validate that scPEFT is competitive with recent PEFT approaches and highlight its robustness  and  effectiveness  for  single-cell  tasks.  Furthermore,  scPEFT  is  designed  as  an extensible framework, and we have integrated support for both OFT and HRA to allow flexible adapter configurations for diverse use cases. As a modular PEFT framework for scLLMs, scPEFT is compatible with emerging techniques and will continue to evolve with future developments in the field. We are committed to maintaining and expanding scPEFT to support more downstream tasks and benefit the broader single-cell research community.", "contextualized_text": "Harnessing the power of single-cell large language models with parameter-efficient fine-tuning using scPEFT\nSupplementary Notes\nSupplementary Note 12: Comparisons with state-of-the-art PEFT techniques\nThese results validate that scPEFT is competitive with recent PEFT approaches and highlight its robustness  and  effectiveness  for  single-cell  tasks.  Furthermore,  scPEFT  is  designed  as  an extensible framework, and we have integrated support for both OFT and HRA to allow flexible adapter configurations for diverse use cases. As a modular PEFT framework for scLLMs, scPEFT is compatible with emerging techniques and will continue to evolve with future developments in the field. We are committed to maintaining and expanding scPEFT to support more downstream tasks and benefit the broader single-cell research community.", "headings": ["Harnessing the power of single-cell large language models with parameter-efficient fine-tuning using scPEFT", "Supplementary Notes", "Supplementary Note 12: Comparisons with state-of-the-art PEFT techniques"], "pages": [19], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/123", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 19, "bbox": {"l": 72.0, "t": 557.98464, "r": 543.2644799999998, "b": 461.1083053259668, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 629]}]}], "headings": ["Harnessing the power of single-cell large language models with parameter-efficient fine-tuning using scPEFT", "Supplementary Notes", "Supplementary Note 12: Comparisons with state-of-the-art PEFT techniques"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 3578786038470275027, "filename": "full_text_02_landing_pdf_link.pdf", "uri": null}}}
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.jsonl:61:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_001358", "chunk_index": 61, "text": "Although  scPEFT  is  designed  to  be  flexible  and  compatible  with  future  scLLMs  because  its adapters operate externally to the backbone models, several practical challenges may still arise. One specific challenge we encountered is the diversity in implementation details and software dependencies  among  existing  scLLMs.  For  instance,  Geneformer  was  developed  using  the Hugging  Face  framework,  which  abstracts  many  low-level  details  such  as  tokenization  and encoder design. This abstraction required extra engineering efforts when attempting to integrate Token  and  Encoder  adapters  from  scPEFT.  In  contrast,  scGPT  and  scFoundation  are implemented using both standard PyTorch Transformer components and a customized version based on FlashAttention. This variation requires programming expertise to properly insert the scPEFT adapters.", "contextualized_text": "Harnessing the power of single-cell large language models with parameter-efficient fine-tuning using scPEFT\nSupplementary Notes\nSupplementary Note 13: Anticipated challenges in extending scPEFT to future scLLMs\nAlthough  scPEFT  is  designed  to  be  flexible  and  compatible  with  future  scLLMs  because  its adapters operate externally to the backbone models, several practical challenges may still arise. One specific challenge we encountered is the diversity in implementation details and software dependencies  among  existing  scLLMs.  For  instance,  Geneformer  was  developed  using  the Hugging  Face  framework,  which  abstracts  many  low-level  details  such  as  tokenization  and encoder design. This abstraction required extra engineering efforts when attempting to integrate Token  and  Encoder  adapters  from  scPEFT.  In  contrast,  scGPT  and  scFoundation  are implemented using both standard PyTorch Transformer components and a customized version based on FlashAttention. This variation requires programming expertise to properly insert the scPEFT adapters.", "headings": ["Harnessing the power of single-cell large language models with parameter-efficient fine-tuning using scPEFT", "Supplementary Notes", "Supplementary Note 13: Anticipated challenges in extending scPEFT to future scLLMs"], "pages": [20], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/126", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 20, "bbox": {"l": 72.0, "t": 673.90464, "r": 543.3505919999999, "b": 533.3483053259669, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 874]}]}], "headings": ["Harnessing the power of single-cell large language models with parameter-efficient fine-tuning using scPEFT", "Supplementary Notes", "Supplementary Note 13: Anticipated challenges in extending scPEFT to future scLLMs"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 3578786038470275027, "filename": "full_text_02_landing_pdf_link.pdf", "uri": null}}}
./data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/chunks/full_2026_07_06_rec_001358.jsonl:62:{"candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_001358", "chunk_index": 62, "text": "In  addition  to  these  technical  considerations,  we  are  observing  a  rapid  development  of  new scLLMs to support a broader range of single-cell applications, such as spatial transcriptomics and multi-omics  data  analysis.  As  the  fields  of  both  scLLMs  and  Parameter-Efficient  Fine-Tuning (PEFT) methods continueessentia to evolve, it will be important to actively update and extend scPEFT to remain compatible with new models and use cases. Despite these challenges, we will be committed to making future scLLMs more accessible for various single-cell applications while reducing the computational burden, thereby benefiting a broader research community, including groups with limited resources.", "contextualized_text": "Harnessing the power of single-cell large language models with parameter-efficient fine-tuning using scPEFT\nSupplementary Notes\nSupplementary Note 13: Anticipated challenges in extending scPEFT to future scLLMs\nIn  addition  to  these  technical  considerations,  we  are  observing  a  rapid  development  of  new scLLMs to support a broader range of single-cell applications, such as spatial transcriptomics and multi-omics  data  analysis.  As  the  fields  of  both  scLLMs  and  Parameter-Efficient  Fine-Tuning (PEFT) methods continueessentia to evolve, it will be important to actively update and extend scPEFT to remain compatible with new models and use cases. Despite these challenges, we will be committed to making future scLLMs more accessible for various single-cell applications while reducing the computational burden, thereby benefiting a broader research community, including groups with limited resources.", "headings": ["Harnessing the power of single-cell large language models with parameter-efficient fine-tuning using scPEFT", "Supplementary Notes", "Supplementary Note 13: Anticipated challenges in extending scPEFT to future scLLMs"], "pages": [20], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/127", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 20, "bbox": {"l": 72.0, "t": 513.8246399999999, "r": 543.1364160000008, "b": 402.3083053259668, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 713]}]}], "headings": ["Harnessing the power of single-cell large language models with parameter-efficient fine-tuning using scPEFT", "Supplementary Notes", "Supplementary Note 13: Anticipated challenges in extending scPEFT to future scLLMs"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 3578786038470275027, "filename": "full_text_02_landing_pdf_link.pdf", "uri": null}}}
